      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "enum\\s+Flag\\b",
          "message": "Declares a Flag enum"
        },
        {
          "pattern": "\\bswitch\\b",
          "message": "Uses a switch expression to explain a flag"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Pair each expense with its flags, then keep the pairs that have any: `expenses.Select(e => new FlaggedExpense(e, CheckPolicy(e))).Where(f => f.Flags.Count > 0).ToList()`.",
        "`Explain` is `flag switch { Flag.MealOverLimit => $\"meal over {MealLimit:N2}\", Flag.LargeExpense => $\"over {LargeExpenseLimit:N2}\", _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(flag)) }`.",
        "Join the reasons with `string.Join(\", \", flagged.Flags.Select(Explain))` and print `$\"  {expense.Date:yyyy-MM-dd} {expense.Category} {expense.Amount:N2}: {reasons}\"`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Patterns that look deeper",
      "body": [
        "A property pattern can look **inside** a property. Every `DateOnly` knows its day of the week, and a pattern can reach it with a dot:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "if (expense is { Category: Category.Travel, Date.DayOfWeek: DayOfWeek.Saturday or DayOfWeek.Sunday })",
        "    flags.Add(Flag.WeekendTravel);",
        "```",
        "",
        "`DayOfWeek` is an enum that comes with .NET. `Saturday or Sunday` is an `or` pattern: either value matches.",
        "",
        "Not every rule needs a pattern. Use whatever reads most plainly:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(expense.Description))",
        "    flags.Add(Flag.MissingDescription);",
        "```",
        "",
        "Adding a rule now takes three small edits: a new member in `Flag`, a new `if` in `CheckPolicy`, and a new arm in `Explain`. Nothing else in the program changes. That is the payoff for keeping *what is flagged* apart from *how it is printed*."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "7 March 2026 was a Saturday. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var date = new DateOnly(2026, 3, 7);",
        "",
        "Console.WriteLine(date.DayOfWeek);",
